# Sandboxing and the Dock

Some code you'd rather not trust with everything — a scraper, a third-party
toolkit, a snippet an agent wrote. A [`sandbox`](/language/kinds-reference) block is
how a workbook runs that code **with only the powers you grant it**. It compiles to
WebAssembly and runs against a single, audited host surface called the **Dock**.

## The membrane

WebAssembly has no ambient authority: a wasm module can compute, but it can't open a
socket, read a file, or call an API on its own. The only things it can do are the
functions the host *imports into it*. The Dock is that host surface — the one place
the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus) hands real powers to guest code.

Nothing crosses the membrane that you didn't grant. That's the whole security model:
not a list of rules the guest is asked to follow, but a wall it physically cannot
reach through.

## What the Dock can offer

The Dock exposes a small, fixed set of host functions. Guest code never gets the raw
power — it gets a narrow, typed call that the host brokers:

- **net** — an HTTP fetch the host performs on the guest's behalf. The host runs an
  SSRF gate first, so a sandbox can't reach loopback, private, or link-local
  addresses even if it asks.
- **kv** — a tenant-scoped key/value store (`get` / `put`). Each tenant sees only
  its own keys.
- **secrets** — read a named secret by key. The *value* never appears in source; the
  guest asks for `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` and the host resolves it (see
  [Secrets & configuration](/deploy/secrets-and-config)).
- **fs** — read/write within the mounted work area, not the host filesystem.
- **exec** — run a registered command. This is how [toolkits](/tooling/toolkits)
  become callable from inside the box.
- **llm** — a brokered model completion.

Each capability is WIT-typed: the host declares the exact function signature the
guest may import, and the weave generates that interface. There's no way to import a
power that wasn't granted.

## Why broker instead of allow

Every Dock function is the host *doing the work*, not the host *opening a hole*. `net`
isn't "raw sockets, please be careful" — it's "ask the host to fetch this URL," and
the host applies its own policy (the SSRF gate, allowlists) before it acts. That
keeps the dangerous part on the trusted side of the membrane, where it can be audited
in exactly one place.

## You declare what it needs

A sandbox names the capabilities it wants, and the weave checks them: `work check`
audits every block against what it actually requests, so a sandbox that asks for
`net` is visible at review time and a sandbox that asks for nothing can't surprise
you. The grants are part of the source — see
[Grants & capabilities](/capabilities/grants-and-capabilities).

The point: untrusted code is still *useful* code. The Dock lets you run it, see
exactly what it can touch, and trust the wall instead of the author.
